\subsection{CS HawkesNet}\label{sec:CS_sim}

Next, we consider our CS HawkesNet, where $k\sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda_{\text{nodes}})$ nodes are added at each event, with the edges added as independent logistic regressions on change statistics. Every edge is considered at every time step, and because the influence of edges on the overall intensity decays in time, criticality (a full graph) is improbable. This allows for transitive closure, but retains identifiability of network parameters.

Parameters were again chosen to achieve an average degree of around $2$, however, with a strong tendency toward transitive closure, as is typical to social networks. We also employ the positive 2-star and negative 3-star pattern as is often observed when fitting related models \cite{Fellows2018,snijders2006}. We also note, in this canonical form the model is over parametrized. Since the change statistics have and edge term, there is a baseline for each edge formation, as well as the multiplication of $K$ on on the triggering kernel, as a result identifiability is lost. We remedy this by fixing $K = 0.5$ and remind users that the temporal point process parameters should be interpreted together with the parameters of the mark kernel.

